EXT. FLOODED PARKING STRUCTURE - TOP DECK - DAWN

Gray water laps at the concrete ramps below. The top deck is an island: a few abandoned cars, a rusted shopping cart, a blue tarp slung between two sedans. The sky is the color of dishwater. Gulls wheel over the drowned city.

Under the tarp, LOUISA KARAGEORGIOU (40s, broad-shouldered, parka tied at the waist) feeds a small fire in a hubcap. Across from her, her daughter, WILLOW KARAGEORGIOU (15, hood up, a knife on her belt), sharpens a stick with careful strokes.

LOUISA
Careful. You'll lose a finger.

WILLOW
I won't.

LOUISA
You don't know that.

WILLOW
I know my fingers.

A scrape of metal on the ramp. They both freeze. Louisa raises her hand, flat. Willow lowers the stick. The scrape comes again, closer, then a splash, then a ragged cough.

A MAN crawls up over the ledge, soaked, shaking, one arm hooked over the concrete. He is gaunt and gray-bearded, a ring of salt crusted around his lips. He drags himself onto the deck and collapses.

LOUISA
(whispering)
Stay behind me.

WILLOW
I'm not scared.

LOUISA
I didn't say you were.

Louisa crosses to the man. Crouches. Turns him over with the toe of her boot. He gasps, blinks up at her.

MAN
(rasping)
Water.

LOUISA
Who are you?

MAN
Please.

LOUISA
Your name.

MAN
Ezra. Ezra Ng-Halloran. I've been swimming since midnight.

LOUISA
From where?

EZRA
The harbor. The ferry sank.

WILLOW
(from behind her mother)
There's no ferry.

EZRA
There was. Yesterday.

Louisa and Willow exchange a look. Louisa unscrews her canteen and holds it out. He drinks like a drowning man. She takes it away after three swallows.

LOUISA
That's enough.

EZRA
Thank you.

LOUISA
Don't thank me yet. Are you alone?

EZRA
Yes.

WILLOW
He's lying.

EZRA
I'm not--

WILLOW
He looked at the stairwell.

All three turn their heads toward the stairwell door. It is closed. The wind rattles its handle.

LOUISA
(very quiet)
Willow. Get the tarp rope.

WILLOW
Why?

LOUISA
Just get it.

Willow scrambles for the rope. Louisa stands, drawing a flare gun from her coat. Ezra holds up both hands, palms out, eyes wide.

EZRA
Please, it's not what you think.

LOUISA
What do I think?

The stairwell door rattles harder, then bursts open. A WOMAN in a dripping yellow slicker staggers out, a gun in her fist. The four of them stand in a tight, shuddering ring, no one breathing.

WOMAN
(shouting)
Ezra! You stupid, stupid man!

EZRA
(weak, relieved)
Marguerite.

She lowers the gun. Looks at Louisa. At Willow. Her shoulders sag.

MARGUERITE
We saw smoke.

LOUISA
So did everyone.

MARGUERITE
We have a boat. We have room.

Louisa lowers the flare gun, slowly. Willow lets out a breath. The gulls cry. Nobody smiles, but the fire burns a little higher.
